## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where callers of the public plugin-sdk SSRF helper `assertHttpUrlTargetsPrivateNetwork` would receive an uncaught `TypeError: Invalid URL` when given malformed input, instead of a controlled application error. Node's `ERR_INVALID_URL` retains the rejected URL in its structured `input` property, so attaching the native error as `cause` would leave credential-bearing endpoint strings reachable to callers that inspect or serialize the complete error graph.

## Why This Change Was Made

The helper now wraps `new URL(url)` in try/catch and throws a sanitized `TypeError("Invalid URL")` without attaching the native parser error or its credential-bearing `input` property. Preserving the `TypeError` class maintains the existing public plugin SDK error contract — callers may use `instanceof TypeError` to distinguish malformed endpoint input from other error classes. This follows the non-disclosing defensive pattern already used by sibling helpers in the same file (for example `isHttpsUrlAllowedByHostnameSuffixAllowlist`). Valid HTTP/HTTPS policy checks are unchanged.

## User Impact

Plugin and channel code that validates self-hosted HTTP endpoints now gets a safe, predictable `TypeError` for malformed URLs without echoing credential-bearing endpoint strings into setup diagnostics, logs, or structured error inspection. Existing `instanceof TypeError` guards continue to work.

## Evidence

**Before (upstream/main):**
`new URL(malformedInput)` throws `TypeError` with the rejected URL preserved in `error.input`.

**Premise (sibling pattern in the same file):**
`isHttpsUrlAllowedByHostnameSuffixAllowlist` already wraps `new URL(url)` in try/catch without reflecting the supplied URL.

**After (this branch) — complete error graph proof:**

```
error name: TypeError
error message: Invalid URL
error.cause: undefined
cause is undefined: true
own property names: [ 'stack', 'message' ]
JSON.stringify(own props): {"message":"Invalid URL"}
serialized includes username: false
serialized includes password: false
```

Credential-bearing malformed input (`http://matrix-user:matrix-fixture@invalid host`) rejects with a sanitized `TypeError` carrying message `Invalid URL` only. No `cause` property is attached; `JSON.stringify` of own error properties contains neither the username nor password substring.

Harness (real exported function, no mocks):
```bash
node --import tsx -e "
import { assertHttpUrlTargetsPrivateNetwork } from './src/plugin-sdk/ssrf-policy.ts';
const malformed = 'http://matrix-user:matrix-fixture@invalid host';
try {
  await assertHttpUrlTargetsPrivateNetwork(malformed, { dangerouslyAllowPrivateNetwork: true });
} catch (err) {
  console.log('error name:', err?.constructor?.name);
  console.log('error message:', err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err));
  const e = err as Error & { cause?: unknown };
  console.log('error.cause:', e.cause);
  console.log('cause is undefined:', e.cause === undefined);
  const entries = Object.entries(Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptors(err));
  console.log('own property names:', entries.map(([k]) => k));
  const serialized = JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(entries.map(([k, d]) => [k, d.value])));
  console.log('JSON.stringify(own props):', serialized);
  console.log('serialized includes username:', serialized.includes('matrix-user'));
  console.log('serialized includes password:', serialized.includes('matrix-fixture'));
}
"
```

Focused tests (includes `TypeError` class assertion):
```bash
node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run src/plugin-sdk/ssrf-policy.test.ts
# 63 passed (includes malformed URL TypeError and credential-reflection tests)
```

Formatting / lint clean:
```bash
./node_modules/.bin/oxfmt --check src/plugin-sdk/ssrf-policy.ts src/plugin-sdk/ssrf-policy.test.ts
# All matched files use the correct format.
node scripts/run-oxlint.mjs --tsconfig config/tsconfig/oxlint.core.json src/plugin-sdk/ssrf-policy.ts src/plugin-sdk/ssrf-policy.test.ts
# (clean)
```

What was not tested: live Matrix/SearXNG/Brave channel setup flows with malformed homeserver URLs; the local harness proves the public SDK entry now fails closed with a sanitized `TypeError`, with no native parser cause attached and no credential-bearing input reachable through structured error inspection or serialization.

AI-assisted: Cursor Composer; proof run manually on this machine.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes #102313.

Under a non-default per-peer DM session scope, two Matrix users whose MXIDs differ only in case resolve to the same DM session key, so the second user loads and reads the first user's private DM transcript with the bot.

Matrix MXIDs are opaque and case-sensitive. The direct branch of the session-key builder force-lowercased the peer id before building the key, so `@Alice:hs.example` and `@alice:hs.example` collapsed onto one key and one persisted transcript. The group/room path does not have this problem: #75670 / #87366 added case preservation, but only for `channel`/`group` peer kinds, and explicitly left direct peers folded.

Exposure needs two preconditions: an operator opted into a non-default `session.dmScope`, and a homeserver that permits case-distinct MXIDs as distinct users (standard Synapse canonicalizes localparts at registration, so it does not fire there).

## Why This Change Was Made

`src/routing/session-key.ts` direct branch, before:

```ts
peerId = normalizeLowercaseStringOrEmpty(peerId);
```

The group branch of the same function instead routes peer ids through `normalizeSessionPeerId`, which honors the `CASE_PRESERVING_PEERS` registry in `src/sessions/session-key-utils.ts`. After:

```ts
peerId = normalizeSessionPeerId({
  channel: params.channel,
  peerKind: "direct",
  peerId,
});
```

plus enrolling `direct` in the Matrix descriptor. That alone is not sufficient, because the registry drives two layers and `normalizeStoreSessionKey` re-folds the key on every session-store lookup. Two further surfaces were required:

1. **Account-scoped DM shape.** Store canonicalization only matched a `<channel>:<peerKind>:` head, so `agent:<id>:matrix:<account>:direct:<peer>` never matched and still folded. Its span regex now tolerates the account segment, matching how `parseSessionDeliveryRoute` already disambiguates that shape.

2. **Alias proof target.** Folded-alias proof compared the session key against *room* delivery targets. A DM key embeds the peer MXID, not the room it was delivered in, so a room target can never appear in it. Left unchanged, this rejected every exact Matrix DM entry and broke DM session continuity outright (caught by `extensions/matrix/src/matrix/monitor/handler.test.ts`). DM keys are now proven against peer identity (`origin.from` / `origin.nativeDirectUserId`) instead, which is also what keeps a genuinely case-distinct sibling from being adopted as a legacy alias.

3. **Alias proof must recognize the account-scoped DM key.** `requiresFoldedSessionKeyAliasProof` read the peer kind positionally as the segment right after the channel, so `agent:<id>:matrix:<account>:direct:<peer>` resolved no descriptor and reported that no proof was needed. The folded alias was then adopted permissively, so under `per-account-channel-peer` an arriving `@Alice` both read `@alice`'s private transcript and had `@alice`'s session key handed back as a legacy key to delete. It now resolves the descriptor through the same account-scoped `direct`/`dm` shape `parseSessionDeliveryRoute` already disambiguates.

### Why this is the right boundary

The case policy stays in the one registry that already owns it; the direct branch stops being the odd path out. Sibling surfaces:

- **Signal** is the other enrolled channel and is deliberately *not* changed here. Per the maintainer question on #102313, Signal direct ids need their case-sensitivity contract proven first; a regression test pins Signal direct as folded.
- **Case-insensitive channels** (Telegram, Discord numeric ids) are correct to fold and keep folding; a regression test pins that.
- **Legacy lowercased DM rows** are adopted on upgrade via the existing folded-alias machinery, now given the correct proof target and taught the account-scoped key shape, so a returning peer keeps their transcript while a case-distinct sibling's row is neither adopted nor deleted. Covered across both DM scopes and both case directions.

### Known limitation

`per-peer` keys are `agent:<id>:direct:<peerId>` and are channel-agnostic by design ("isolate by sender, across channels"), so they carry no channel to enroll and cannot preserve case without either changing a persisted key shape or changing folding for every channel. `per-peer` therefore still folds; `per-channel-peer` (the documented recommended value) and `per-account-channel-peer` are fixed. This is documented in `docs/concepts/session.md` and a regression test pins the current behavior.

## User Impact

Operators using `session.dmScope: per-channel-peer` or `per-account-channel-peer` on Matrix no longer risk one peer's DM transcript being served to a different, case-distinct peer. No config change is needed and existing DM sessions are preserved. Behavior on every other channel, on the default `main` scope, and on the group/room path is unchanged.

## Evidence

### Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/sessions/session-key-case-preservation.test.ts` — 60 passed. The new cases fail on pristine main; the negative controls that pin Signal/Telegram folding pass on both, as intended. Reverting only the account-scoped alias-proof resolution fails exactly the two account-scoped cases and nothing else.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/routing src/config/sessions src/sessions` — 1675 passed.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/matrix/src/matrix/monitor/handler.test.ts` — 121 passed, including the DM-collision case that caught the continuity regression described above.
- `oxlint` and `oxfmt --check` clean on all changed files; `run-tsgo.mjs -p tsconfig.core.json` reports no errors in any changed file.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: under a per-peer Matrix DM scope, two case-distinct MXIDs share one session key, so the second peer loads the first peer's persisted DM transcript.
Real environment tested: the production `buildAgentSessionKey` and `resolveSessionStoreEntry` were driven directly, first on pristine base b319493a52 and then on the patched tree at 9c2f5eff14, with identical inputs. Nothing was faked: the real key builder, the real store canonicalizer, and the real folded-alias resolver all ran, over an in-memory session store holding realistic Matrix DM metadata (delivery target is the DM room, peer MXID on origin.from). No external seam was stubbed; this path performs no network calls.
Exact steps or command run after this patch: for each of dmScope `per-channel-peer` and `per-account-channel-peer`, build the DM session keys for `@Alice:hs.example` and `@alice:hs.example`, then resolve the store entry three ways on identical inputs: a store holding only `@Alice`'s session with an incoming `@alice` turn, the mirror (a store holding only `@alice`'s session with an incoming `@Alice` turn), and a store holding only `@Alice`'s own lowercased legacy row with an incoming `@Alice` turn. Report which transcript is handed over, whether the other peer's key is returned for deletion, and whether the peer keeps their own legacy row.
Evidence after fix:
```
# BEFORE (pristine base b319493a52)
dmScope=per-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: Alice-private-transcript
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice loads: alice-private-transcript
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript

dmScope=per-account-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: Alice-private-transcript
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice loads: alice-private-transcript
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: true
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript

# AFTER (this patch 9c2f5eff14, identical inputs)
dmScope=per-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@Alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: (none - new session)
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: false
  @Alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice loads: (none - new session)
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: false
  @alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript

dmScope=per-account-channel-peer
  @Alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@Alice:hs.example
  @alice key: agent:main:matrix:acct1:direct:@alice:hs.example
  @alice loads: (none - new session)
  @alice reads @Alice's private DM history: false
  @Alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice loads: (none - new session)
  @Alice reads @alice's private DM history: false
  @alice's session marked for deletion: false
  @Alice keeps own legacy row on upgrade: Alice-legacy-transcript
```
Observed result after fix: on the pristine base both MXIDs collapse onto one identical DM session key and each peer is handed the other's private transcript in both directions and under both scopes. With the patch every one of those reads is refused, the two peers get distinct keys and each starts a clean session of their own, neither peer's session is returned for deletion, and a returning peer still resolves to their own prior lowercased row. An earlier revision of this branch closed only the first direction under `per-account-channel-peer`; that gap is what the account-scoped alias-proof resolution above fixes, and the mirror-direction line is the one that moves.
What was not tested: no live homeserver was staged, so the full inbound-to-transcript-load flow was not exercised end to end against a real Matrix server; the proof drives the real key builder and the real store resolver that decide which transcript loads. The `per-peer` scope is out of scope by design and still folds. Signal direct ids are unchanged pending their identity-contract proof. A full packaged build was not run, as no dynamic-import, packaging, or published surface changed.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where a failed Kilocode model discovery response could leave its response body unread while OpenClaw falls back to the static Kilocode catalog.

## Why This Change Was Made

The non-OK discovery branch now cancels the response body before returning the fallback catalog, matching the cleanup behavior used by sibling provider discovery paths.

## User Impact

Kilocode setup/model refresh failures keep the existing fallback behavior without holding a failed HTTP response stream open.

## Evidence

- Standalone local streaming-server proof: `node --import tsx proof-live.mjs` imports the production `discoverKilocodeModels()` module, routes only the fixed Kilocode URL's network boundary to a `node:http` loopback server, returns HTTP 500 with a streaming body, verifies the static catalog fallback, and prints the server-side failed response `close` event after fallback. The full `proof-live.mjs` source is included below.
- Local HTTP server regression: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/kilocode/provider-models.test.ts -- -t "falls back to static catalog on HTTP error"` passed with the committed loopback regression.
- Full focused suite: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/kilocode/provider-models.test.ts` passed 10 tests.

```text
$ node --import tsx proof-live.mjs
[kilocode-models] Failed to discover models: HTTP 500, using static catalog
entrypoint: extensions/kilocode/provider-models.ts discoverKilocodeModels()
dependency-boundary: local node:http streaming server for fixed Kilocode URL
server-status: 500
fallback-model: kilo-auto/balanced
model-count: 1
response-close-observed: yes
server-write-count-before-close: 2
guard-release-called: yes

$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/kilocode/provider-models.test.ts -- -t "falls back to static catalog on HTTP error"
Test Files  1 passed (1)
Tests  1 passed | 9 skipped (10)

$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs extensions/kilocode/provider-models.test.ts
Test Files  1 passed (1)
Tests  10 passed (10)
```

The local-server proof is committed in `extensions/kilocode/provider-models.test.ts`: the server returns a non-OK streaming response, `discoverKilocodeModels()` returns the unchanged static Kilocode catalog, and the test asserts the server observed the failed response close.

<details>
<summary>standalone proof source</summary>

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
import { createServer } from "node:http";
import { registerHooks } from "node:module";
import { setTimeout as delay } from "node:timers/promises";

function listenOnLoopback(server) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    server.once("error", reject);
    server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", () => {
      server.off("error", reject);
      const address = server.address();
      if (!address || typeof address === "string") {
        reject(new Error("expected local server to listen on a TCP port"));
        return;
      }
      resolve(`http://127.0.0.1:${address.port}/models`);
    });
  });
}

function waitForClose(timeoutMs) {
  return Promise.race([
    globalThis.__kilocodeProofResponseClosed,
    delay(timeoutMs).then(() => {
      throw new Error("timed out waiting for failed response close");
    }),
  ]);
}

delete process.env.NODE_ENV;
delete process.env.VITEST;

let closeObserved = false;
let writeCount = 0;
let releaseCalled = false;
let resolveResponseClosed;
globalThis.__kilocodeProofResponseClosed = new Promise((resolve) => {
  resolveResponseClosed = resolve;
});

const server = createServer((_request, response) => {
  response.writeHead(500, { "Content-Type": "text/plain" });
  response.write("temporary failure\n");
  writeCount += 1;
  const interval = setInterval(() => {
    writeCount += 1;
    response.write("stream body that should not be fully read\n");
  }, 10);
  response.on("close", () => {
    clearInterval(interval);
    closeObserved = true;
    resolveResponseClosed();
  });
});

const localModelsUrl = await listenOnLoopback(server);
globalThis.__kilocodeProofLocalModelsUrl = localModelsUrl;
globalThis.__kilocodeProofRelease = () => {
  releaseCalled = true;
};

registerHooks({
  resolve(specifier, context, nextResolve) {
    if (specifier === "openclaw/plugin-sdk/ssrf-runtime") {
      return {
        url:
          "data:text/javascript," +
          encodeURIComponent(`
export async function fetchWithSsrFGuard(params) {
  const response = await fetch(globalThis.__kilocodeProofLocalModelsUrl, params.init);
  return {
    response,
    finalUrl: globalThis.__kilocodeProofLocalModelsUrl,
    release: async () => globalThis.__kilocodeProofRelease(),
  };
}

export function ssrfPolicyFromHttpBaseUrlAllowedHostname(baseUrl) {
  return { allowedHostnames: [new URL(baseUrl).hostname] };
}
`),
        shortCircuit: true,
      };
    }
    return nextResolve(specifier, context);
  },
});

try {
  const { discoverKilocodeModels } = await import("./extensions/kilocode/provider-models.ts");
  const models = await discoverKilocodeModels();
  await waitForClose(1000);
  const fallback = models.find((model) => model.id === "kilo-auto/balanced");

  console.log("entrypoint: extensions/kilocode/provider-models.ts discoverKilocodeModels()");
  console.log("dependency-boundary: local node:http streaming server for fixed Kilocode URL");
  console.log("server-status: 500");
  console.log(`fallback-model: ${fallback?.id ?? "missing"}`);
  console.log(`model-count: ${models.length}`);
  console.log(`response-close-observed: ${closeObserved ? "yes" : "no"}`);
  console.log(`server-write-count-before-close: ${writeCount}`);
  console.log(`guard-release-called: ${releaseCalled ? "yes" : "no"}`);

  if (!fallback || !closeObserved || !releaseCalled || writeCount <= 0) {
    process.exitCode = 1;
  }
} finally {
  server.close();
}
```

</details>

